//Scope life cycle
any-event -> $apply- $digest
$apply will start modifying DOM
$digest will do a comparission between prev and current DOM value and will commit DOM changes. Until $digest step of scope life cycle executes, DOM changes won't be reflected.
        Even if we are calling explicit $watch , it will internally call $digest event, then will commit changes. 
	

1.Creation
      Root scope will be created during bootstaping of an application else during linking of a template.

2.Watcher registration
       During template linking, directives will register watch for the scope, so that as soon as scope changes,DOM should get refacted.

3.Model mutation
       scope.$apply() method will be called. Hence all model changes will start modifying DOM.

4.Mutation observation
       At the end of $apply, Angular performs a $digest cycle on the root scope,which propagates through all child scopes.
 During $digest, all $watch expressions are checked if any DOM change is required and if it is supposed to change the DOM then $watch listener is called.

5.Scope destruction
      When child scopes are no loonger user,its the responsibility of child scope to call scope.$destroy() API, which will destroy the scope 
and all propagation of $digest call will be stopped for the scope then garbage collector is allowed to clean the memory.

//Directive types
1. Observing directive
            {{expression}}, register listeners using the $watch() method. This kind of directive needs to be notified whenever scope changes.
2.Listener directive
            ng-click etc registers a listener in DOM. Whenever the listener is called, $apply() method will be called internally to reflect the DOM.


